A||||12||Luni420||MR2 Turbo timing belt issues  PLEASE HELP if you can||||1||
Z||000000||Luni420||09-26-2001||06:24 PM||||I know its a Fiero board but I am posting this at any board I can. I know after the 3SGTE engine topic Im probabally going to get laughed at but I dont care. I really need help so if any of you know (which most of you seem to know your stuff on cars in general) anything that may help me please let me know. Hers my problem.<P>I just got my 91 MR2 Turbo. Drove it 1800 miles home. Thing ran great. Got it home and within 2 days the timing belt slipped a few slots on the cams. So I took it in to a shop and he said the tensioner pulley was bad so he put a new one on. So I got it back yesterday and the same damn thing happened again. The timing belt slipped again. Now taking mechanic error out of the equation, is there anything that could cause my timing belt to just slip like that, assuming the engine was put back together right (giving him benefit of the doubt). The timing belt was replaced before I bought it. Would putting the old/new belt back on with a new tensioner pulley hurt anything. Would the fact that the belt had been stretched to fit once already affect its ability to stay on. I mean once those things are on, does it hurt to put it back on, or if while its off you have to put a new one back on so it can stretch to fit again. Please help me, as this car is becoming more trouble than its worth, and Im thinking about giving it back to the owner and getting my money back. While Im on that subject, does anyone know a good place online to view local lemon laws applicable to private sales? ANY help would be VERY much appreciated. Thanks. <P>Luni420 <BR>AIM: Lunitari420 <BR>MSN Messanger: Lunitari@hotmail.com <BR>Lunitari@hotmail.com<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||09-26-2001||07:21 PM||||I don't have any specific experience with the MR2 engine, but I did have a similar problem with my '87 VW GTI 16V. I had replaced the timing belt about 6 months earlier (preventive maintenance).  The VW engine uses a "cogged" rubber belt.  The belt is tensioned (by adjusting the cam in the idler pulley).  When adjusted to spec, the belt is tight!  My impression was that there was no way this belt could ever jump a tooth without significant damage to the belt.  Well, one cold and snowy day, about 6 months later, I noticed a marked loss in power.  The ignition timing was way off.  Further checks revealed the timing belt had jumped a tooth.  This was especially unexpected since I was doing alot of low RPM driving (snow and icy roads).  Also, upon inspection, the timing belt showed absolutely no signs of damage.  I know the belt was installed and tensioned correctly.  Everything looked fine, it was still nice and tight, except it was now off by one tooth! I installed another new belt (couldn't trust the one that jumped a tooth) and drove the car another 7K miles or so until I sold it.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierosound||09-26-2001||07:45 PM||||Excuse me...  but isn't there an MR2 board that would be able to help you with this?<BR>Most of us here drive Fieros, and could help you if it was some other GM car...<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000003||Luni420||09-26-2001||09:00 PM||||Yes there is an MR2 board, but you fiero guys seem to know more about cars in general so I thought Id ask you. This board in general has been very helpful to me since I posted on here at first. But I think I have the problem nailed anyway. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||09-26-2001||09:45 PM||||Luni,  I would go back and check your assumption (the eng/belt was assembled correctly) to see if that holds water. Other than that, I hove no experience with Mr2s but would say that I'm sure the engineers knew what they were doing and something is probably wrong with the engine.  BTW why was the seller selling it?  <P>Mark, you belt jump sounds like a classic case of water build up inside the belt chamber, freezing to ice at the bottom (due to gravity of course), and the ice causing the skip of some cogs on the pulley.  The dead give away was snowy & icy roads.<P>So I guess the Mr2 folks don't like you or couldn't help you...  GL||||reg||1
Z||000005||Luni420||09-26-2001||11:43 PM||||No, I posted my question on this board, the Celica board, and the MR2 board. I was just trying to get the broadest selection of answers possible. Thats why I posted here and everywhere else. Theres always someone smart on every board thats willing to take a shot at it. Thats all I wanted, just some ideas to see if any of them matched with what I was thinking. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroMaster88||09-27-2001||12:02 AM||||Glad to see we have polite members on this forum <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>I would say check your timing belt to see if it is stretched.  or see if some ribs are broken off.  Just a thought.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">              <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000007||GodSend||09-27-2001||01:29 AM||||Had a similar problem with my Eagle Talon.<P>Replaced BOTH timing belts, and asked the mechanic to replace the waterpump (right behind the belts) ... well, turns out he dident, and 6 months later - guess what - water pump went.<P>Since I just spent 900$ on the new timing belts (and tensioner) I dident want to replace them, so I just got the pump done.  2 weeks later one of the pullies for the tbelt froze and it jumped a tooth (you could see where the belt stuck to the pully).<P>Replaced the pully, dealer agreed to replace the tbelts for free - and I sold it shortly there after.<P>If a belt gets any sort of contaminates on it (oil, greese, coolant) then replace it - this could be the case?||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||09-27-2001||03:08 AM||||AAAck, is the 3GSTE an interfearance engine? (meaning pistions will HIT the valves if they are extended at TDC?)  If so, do NOT drive the car. (it might not be interfearance, as turbo motors generally have larger combustioon chamber volume due to lower compression for boost.)<P>Timing belt wise, they are really fit-once items.  If they are EVER removed after the engine has been started, for any reason, they must be thrown away, and new ones put on, sorry.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Mk.2 Turbos are great cars, hope you enjoy yours once it's back running strong!!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||Luni420||09-27-2001||04:58 AM||||No its a non interference motor. Its ok. Im thinking it needs a new water pump, pulleys, and timing belt. After that it should be strong as ever. I hope. Thanks guys.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||09-27-2001||09:04 AM||||As for the timing belt, everyones answers above covers most possibilities. The only other thing I can think of, is the timing belt area getting damp? like does the water pump leak? Antifreeze can be used as a lubricant and damage timing belts. The other thing is the new belt an aftermarket or OEM part? I have seen some aftermarket parts that sucked in quality. If everything is dry, I'd see about getting a timing belt from Toyota. As strange as it sounds it may solve your problem. You could also ask on the MR2 board if they have noticed if any brand of aftermarket belts have high failures or problems.<BR> As for Lemon Law, It is only applicable to car manufacturers and dealers on New cars. Used and privately owned/sold vehicles are not covered under the law. The "Buyer Beware" is the only law in that area.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000011||falconhulk||09-27-2001||09:17 AM||||If you are using aftermarket belts you are crazy!  There are kits to convert to chains anyway with musch longer life.  I would go with that option if it was me.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Luni420||09-27-2001||04:55 PM||||Where can I find such a kit?||||reg||1
